# Exercise: Verify L = λW
#
# Little's Law: L = λW. The chapter's centerpiece. Now we MEASURE
# all three quantities from a real run and check they line up.
#
# A useful algebraic identity:
#
#     L = λ × W_avg
#       = (N / elapsed) × (sum_W / N)
#       = sum_W / elapsed
#
# where sum_W is the total time-in-system across all N items. So
# instead of measuring λ and W separately and multiplying (with
# integer-division headaches), we measure sum_W and elapsed and
# divide once at the end.
#
# Setup: 1 worker, 3 items at 20ms each (serial; nothing fancy).
#   Item i (1-indexed) enters at t=0, leaves at t = i × 20ms.
#   Predicted W's: 20, 40, 60 ms. sum_W = 120 ms.
#   Predicted elapsed: 60 ms.
#   Predicted L = 120 / 60 = 2.
#
# We assert 1 <= L <= 3 — a generous window for jitter and
# integer-division rounding.
#
# Notes on the pattern below:
#   - `time.now` captures a microsecond timestamp.
#   - `i./` returns ( quotient Bool ) — drop the bool.
#   - Per-receive accumulator pattern:
#         <copy r> chan.receive drop drop      \ ack receive
#         time.now <copy start> i.-            \ that item's W
#         swap i.+                              \ fold into running sum
#
# Your task: measure sum_W and elapsed, divide to get L, leave it
# on top. The boilerplate is set up — fill in the per-receive line.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-verify-law ( -- )
    chan.make
    chan.make

    [ over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      over chan.receive drop 20 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
      drop drop ] strand.spawn drop

    time.now                            # ( w r start )

    2 pick 1 swap chan.send drop
    2 pick 2 swap chan.send drop
    2 pick 3 swap chan.send drop

    0                                   # ( w r start sum_W=0 )

    # Stack going in: ( w r start sum_W )
    # Your code here — three lines, one per receive. Each line:
    # - uses 2 pick on r to receive (drop drop the value+Bool)
    # - takes time.now, subtracts start, that's W_i for this item
    # - folds W_i into the running sum
    # After three receives, stack should be: ( w r start sum_W )
    drop drop drop drop 0 0 0 0    # placeholder — replace this

    time.now 2 pick i.-                 # ( w r start sum_W elapsed )
    i./ drop                            # ( w r start L )
    nip nip nip                         # ( L )

    # Do not edit below this line
    # L for 1 worker / 3 items / 20ms should be ≈ 2
    dup 1 i.>= test.assert
    3 i.<= test.assert
;
